WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:02.420
All right. How to chain conditionals.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.120
This one is super important because in order to work with conditional reasoning

3
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.780
profitably during the exam, yes, of course, you do have to know how to diagram it.

4
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.080
That's a given. But you have to be able to chain your conditionals

5
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.240
together to get

6
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.010
probably the majority, I think majority is fair to say, the majority of conditional

7
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.840
questions correct. Or at least some strong

8
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.360
percentage.

9
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.900
Because a lot of questions regarding conditional reasoning test your

10
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.589
ability to chain conditionals and see the relationships between conditional

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.520
statements. And so in order to be good at chaining conditionals, you're going to

12
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.860
have to listen really closely to this next thing that I'm about to say.

13
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.000
It's so, so important. If you have one rule, one thing you remember from this

14
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.860
video, it should be to chain as you

15
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.280
go. Chain conditionals as you go. As you diagram

16
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.820
your statements, which you should be doing in the quip stage of your translation as

17
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.660
you go through, you want to have a good enough conceptual awareness

18
00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.640
via your translation of all the variables that have been said so far to the

19
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.600
point where you are able to, when you get a new statement, immediately

20
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.600
know, "Oh,

21
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.100
that was just said. There's an interlocking variable here.

22
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.980
Okay. I should just write this new statement onto the

23
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:18.760
old one."

24
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.270
And I'm going to, of course, show you how to do that in a second, but I just want

25
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.380
to introduce it so you get the general concept.

26
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.940
And not diagram it separately and then at the end of the stimulus be

27
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.820
like, "Well, once I see everything separately, that's when I'll

28
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.400
figure out, okay, are there any connections here?

29
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.520
Are there any chaining opportunities yet? Then I'll do it." No, you won't.

30
00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.460
At least not reliably.

31
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.560
People often give themselves this debt of like, "At the end of the

32
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.150
stimulus, I'm going to chain it all together," and very rarely are those debts

33
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.980
paid 100% of the time.

34
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.120
And what I want is 100% of the time those conditionals get chained, not just

35
00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.640
when you remember or when it's convenient, but every single time.

36
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.480
That's why chaining has to be really intrinsic to your diagramming

37
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.610
process. All right. I want to show you how this actually works, and then we'll get

38
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.880
more into the nitty-gritty of it. All right.

39
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.800
So let's diagram these conditionals together first.

40
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.880
And I'm going to go one sentence at a time.

41
